INT. CHURCH BELL TOWER - DAY

Snow drifts through the broken louvres and settles on the great bronze bell like flour on a baker's arm. The wooden stair groans below. The air smells of pigeon and old rope.

SERGEANT MAGNUS HADLEY, 40s, a face scoured by wind, crouches at the north louvre with binoculars. PRIVATE QUIGG, 19, freckled, hugs a machine gun. PRIVATE NASRIN KHALIL, 20s, a field radio strapped to her back, picks at a frozen knot. CORPORAL TOMASZ WIERZBICKI, 30s, sits against the wall, bandaging his own hand.

QUIGG
They're moving in the orchard.

HADLEY
Count.

NASRIN
Nine? Ten --

QUIGG
Twelve. I said twelve.

TOMASZ
(muttering)
Twelve, nine, who cares, we're up a steeple.

HADLEY
Quiet. Both of you.

A footstep on the stair. Slow. Four rifles swing.

MOTHER AGATHE, 70s, a black habit and a snow-crusted shawl, climbs into the loft carrying a kettle. She is breathing hard and not remotely afraid.

MOTHER AGATHE
You have been in my bell tower since Tuesday. I thought you might want tea.

NASRIN
Mother, you can't be up here --

QUIGG
Is it safe? Did you check it?

TOMASZ
She's a nun, she's not going to poison us.

MOTHER AGATHE
Not before the second cup.

Hadley lowers the binoculars. For the first time all day, he laughs.
